From: fish <fish@bc250-fsr4-research>
Date: Sun, 7 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 8/8] radv: BC-250 FSR4 resolution-variant coverage and 8K store guard

Opt-in extension of the FSR4 optimization to the shader buckets outside
the one 0006/0007 already covered, from the 2026-09-07 research bundle.
Both parts are off unless BC250_FSR4_RESOLUTION_VARIANTS=1.

FSR4 ships separate shaders per output-resolution bucket, so the earlier
work only ever matched the middle one; 1080p and 4K+ silently got no
uplift because their shaders never matched an identity. This adds 24
further shader identities covering those buckets, each tagged with
flag bit 0x40000000 so they are ignored entirely unless the switch is on.

It also adds bc250_resolution_guard_spirv(), which is a correctness fix
rather than an optimization. The pinned INT8 8K shaders encode masked
DWORD stores at byte 0x08000000, an address inside their own 332 MB
scratch allocation, where a store can overwrite a live input and race a
neighbouring invocation at clipped edges. For eight exactly identified
bytecodes -- matched by size and FNV-1a hash, and additionally verified
to hold an OpConstant of that value at the expected word before anything
is written -- the constant is rewritten to redirect the store beyond any
scratch buffer this provider supports. It can be enabled on its own with
BC250_FSR4_RESOLUTION_GUARD=1.

Both switches extend the pipeline cache key, so shaders built with and
without them cannot be served to each other.

Upstream notes the 4K+ bucket previously failed to show an uplift and is
curious whether this multi-bucket logic changes that; that is unverified
here. Not measured by us on real hardware.

Origin: bc250-fsr4-discord-20260907 bundle,
upgrade-from-previous/0003-resolution-over-composed.patch
